WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.620
When creating your databases,

2
00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.210
you need to be able to
distinguish between

3
00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.235
different kinds of
database schemas.

4
00:00:05.235 --> 00:00:07.920
In other words, you need
to answer the question of

5
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.755
what kind of database
best suits my project?

6
00:00:10.755 --> 00:00:12.390
Over the next few minutes,

7
00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:15.210
you'll explore some different
types of database schemas.

8
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.845
By the end of this video,

9
00:00:16.845 --> 00:00:18.090
you'll be able to explain

10
00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.370
the concept of a logical
database schema,

11
00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:23.670
and outline the concept of
a physical database schema.

12
00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:27.060
Let's begin by exploring a
logical database schema.

13
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.190
A logical database schema is how

14
00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:31.860
the data is organized
in terms of tables.

15
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.660
In other words, it shows

16
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.360
what tables should
be in a database,

17
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:36.920
and explains how
the attributes of

18
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.990
different tables are
linked together.

19
00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:41.705
Creating a logical
database schema means

20
00:00:41.705 --> 00:00:43.040
illustrating relationships

21
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.125
between components of your data.

22
00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:49.730
This is also called entity
relationship or ER modeling.

23
00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.800
It specifies what
the relationships

24
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.690
between entity types are.

25
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.540
Let's take the example of
a simple ER model that

26
00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.525
shows the logical schema of
an ordering application.

27
00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:02.405
It demonstrates the
relationship between an order,

28
00:01:02.405 --> 00:01:04.310
the shipment in which
it will be shipped,

29
00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.310
and the courier assigned to it.

30
00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:09.170
The ID attribute
in each table is

31
00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:12.005
the primary key of the
respective entities.

32
00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:14.870
It provides a unique
identifier for each entry,

33
00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:17.305
row, or record in the entities.

34
00:01:17.305 --> 00:01:19.050
In the order entity,

35
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:22.685
the shipment ID and courier
ID are called foreign keys.

36
00:01:22.685 --> 00:01:25.190
But in fact, they are
also the primary keys

37
00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:28.085
of the shipment and courier
entities respectively.

38
00:01:28.085 --> 00:01:30.020
This creates a relation between

39
00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.700
these entities and
the order table,

40
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.315
which in turn has its own
ID as its primary key.

41
00:01:35.315 --> 00:01:38.555
The other type of schema
is physical schema.

42
00:01:38.555 --> 00:01:41.960
Physical schema is how
data is stored in disk.

43
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.480
In other words, this
involves creating

44
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.600
the actual structure of
your database using code.

45
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.735
In my SQL, another
relational databases,

46
00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:52.700
developers use SQL to create

47
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.270
the database tables and the
other database objects.

48
00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:58.190
For example, you can create

49
00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:00.950
a physical schema for an
online store database

50
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.350
by writing SQL statements
to create tables for

51
00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:06.245
customers, products,
and transactions.

52
00:02:06.245 --> 00:02:08.810
However, physical
schema creation could

53
00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:11.825
differ slightly between
different database systems.

54
00:02:11.825 --> 00:02:14.015
Database schemas are vital

55
00:02:14.015 --> 00:02:15.200
when it comes to the creation of

56
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.055
databases and they form the
basis of your application.

57
00:02:19.055 --> 00:02:21.260
You should also be
able to describe how

58
00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.180
a logical database
schema refers to

59
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.460
the organization
of data in tables,

60
00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.320
and that you're
using an ER model to

61
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.225
specify relationships
between entities.

62
00:02:30.225 --> 00:02:32.330
You should also now
note that you can

63
00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.280
control how data is
physically stored

64
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.720
on disk by creating

65
00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.940
a physical schema
with SQL statements.